I deploy OpenBot on my homeserver with a single Ansible role. The role creates a dedicated system user, installs a systemd drop-in that caps memory, and makes the install safe to re-run.

## The role

An Ansible role is a fixed directory layout, and only the directories you use need to exist. For one service I keep four:

```text
roles/openbot/
├── defaults/main.yml     # tunables: user, paths, memory limit
├── tasks/main.yml        # user, files, drop-in, service
├── handlers/main.yml     # daemon-reload + restart
└── meta/main.yml         # optional metadata
```

The reason to put the deploy in a role is idempotency. [Ansible's glossary](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/reference_appendices/glossary.html#term-Idempotency) defines an operation as idempotent when running it once gives the same result as running it repeatedly. A second run creates no duplicate user, no duplicate unit, no duplicate config.

Restarts are where idempotency usually breaks. The role avoids unconditional restarts by doing them only through [handlers](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_handlers.html). A handler runs when a task that notifies it reports `changed`, and if several tasks notify the same handler it still runs once. A run that changes nothing restarts nothing.

This is the provisioning flow on a first run, and what the second run does:

<Mermaid diagram={`
    sequenceDiagram
        participant OP as Operator
        participant PL as Ansible play
        participant US as user task
        participant CP as copy task
        participant HD as Handler
        participant SD as systemd
        OP->>PL: ansible-playbook site.yml
        PL->>US: create service user
        US-->>PL: ok / changed
        PL->>CP: write drop-in
        CP-->>PL: ok / changed
        CP-->>HD: notify
        PL->>HD: run notified handler
        HD->>SD: systemctl daemon-reload
        HD->>SD: restart openbot.service
        Note over PL,SD: second run
        PL->>US: user unchanged
        PL->>CP: drop-in unchanged
        Note over PL,HD: nothing notified, no restart
    `} />

I check it by running the role twice:

```bash
ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff   # what would change
ansible-playbook site.yml                  # first run: applies changes
ansible-playbook site.yml                  # second run: expect changed=0
```

If the second run reports changes, the role is not declarative yet.

## A dedicated user, outside `docker`

OpenBot runs under its own system account. The important part is what that account does not have: membership in the `docker` group.

Docker's docs ([post-install](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/linux-postinstall/) and [security](https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/)) are explicit: the daemon always runs as root, only trusted users should control it, and the `docker` group grants root-level privileges. Anyone who can talk to the daemon can start a container with the host's `/` mounted into it, so daemon access is host access.

A service that never talks to the Docker API should not hold that. The service account is not in `docker`. If a container runtime is involved, the play drives it, not this account.

```yaml
- name: Create the service account (no supplementary groups)
  ansible.builtin.user:
    name: openbot
    system: true
    create_home: true
    shell: /usr/sbin/nologin
    groups: ""
    state: present
```

Two details in the [`user` module](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/collections/ansible/builtin/user_module.html) matter:

- I set `groups: ""`. Omitting the key does not mean "no extra groups": it means Ansible does not manage groups at all, and an account someone added to `docker` stays there. Listing `groups` makes Ansible manage membership and, by default, remove the user from every supplementary group you did not list (the primary group is not affected). The empty string removes all of them, so the account converges to "no extra groups" even if someone added it to `docker` by hand.
- `system: true` cannot be changed on an existing user, so the account has to be created correctly the first time.

## A memory cap, as a systemd drop-in

A runaway process should not take the whole homeserver down. I cap memory at the systemd layer, next to the unit that starts the service.

[`MemoryMax=`](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.resource-control.html) sets an absolute limit on the unit (available since systemd 231). If the processes cannot stay under it, the OOM killer is invoked inside that unit. It is the last line of defense, not the main control: `MemoryHigh=` throttles and reclaims memory instead of killing. I keep `MemoryMax=` as the hard backstop, and reach for `MemoryHigh=` only when throttling is enough.

The limit lives in a drop-in, not in an edit to the unit file:

```plaintext
# /etc/systemd/system/openbot.service.d/10-memory.conf
[Service]
MemoryMax=1G
```

`1G` is a placeholder, not my real value. The directive accepts `K`, `M`, `G`, `T` suffixes or a percentage of installed RAM.

Drop-in files are merged after the main unit file, in alphanumeric order, and `/etc` takes precedence over `/run` and `/usr/lib` ([systemd.unit](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.unit.html)). Overriding only the setting I care about means vendor updates to the rest of the unit still apply; a full replacement unit stops receiving them. An upgrade that ships a new unit does not silently drop my limit, and the limit stays in the role.

<Mermaid diagram={`
    flowchart TD
        V["vendor unit<br/>/usr/lib/systemd/system/openbot.service"] -->|parsed first| M["openbot.service (merged)"]
        D["drop-in<br/>/etc/systemd/system/openbot.service.d/10-memory.conf"] -->|parsed after, wins| M["openbot.service (merged)"]
    `} />

After writing the drop-in, systemd must re-read its configuration. The reload and restart go through a handler, so they only happen when the file changed:

```yaml
# tasks/main.yml
- name: Install the memory-cap drop-in
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    dest: /etc/systemd/system/openbot.service.d/10-memory.conf
    mode: "0644"
    content: |
      [Service]
      MemoryMax=1G
  notify: Reload systemd and restart openbot

# handlers/main.yml
- name: Reload systemd and restart openbot
  ansible.builtin.systemd_service:
    name: openbot
    state: restarted
    daemon_reload: true
```

`systemctl show openbot -p MemoryMax` is a quick way to check the limit systemd actually applied.

## A checklist for the next service

None of this is OpenBot-specific. For any self-hosted app I manage this way:

- One role per service, with `defaults/` holding every tunable.
- One dedicated system user, no supplementary groups, no login shell.
- Config and secrets owned by that user, with modes set explicitly.
- A unit the role installs, or a packaged unit the role only extends.
- Limits and overrides as drop-ins under `/etc/systemd/system/<unit>.d/`.
- Handlers for `daemon-reload` and restart, so a no-op run stays a no-op.
- A second run of the role as the acceptance test: it should change nothing.
